  "result": "\"More agents always give better results\" isn't true, so neither five nor ten is the right starting point. The better question is whether your assistant needs more than one agent.\n\nAnthropic's engineering team built a multi-agent research system and wrote up what they found. Agents use about 4× the tokens of a plain chat, and multi-agent systems use about 15×. Their setup did beat a single agent, but on research tasks with many independent searches that could run in parallel. They also say multi-agent is a poor fit when agents must share the same context or depend heavily on each other's work. ([source](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system))\n\nHere is what that means for your makerspace assistant. Take the request \"Can I use the laser cutter Thursday, and what material is safe?\" It has three steps:\n1. Look up the manual.\n2. Check the calendar.\n3. Book a slot.\n\nEach step needs the result of the one before it, and the whole thing fits in one context. Splitting it across several agents would add handoffs and extra tokens, and your budget is small. A single agent with three tools, or a fixed workflow for the booking step, is the likely starting point.\n\nA second agent starts to pay off when you have a real reason for it. For example, manuals so long that searching them floods the main context, or independent subtasks that can run in parallel.\n\nFor your design review, try this framing: start with one agent, and add another only when you can name the specific problem it solves and what it costs.\n\nQuick check: in the Thursday laser-cutter example, why would splitting the three steps across three agents likely cost more without helping?\n\nI can also write a lesson on when multi-agent pays off, if you'd like one.",
